speakersafetyd for the SiliconForest guest (J293, 13" M1 MacBook Pro)
================================================================================

A port of the Asahi Linux speaker safety daemon, speakersafetyd 2.0.1 (git 4175df66,
https://github.com/AsahiLinux/speakersafetyd, MIT, see LICENSE). The thermal model
and the main loop are upstream's; only the platform layer is new: instead of ALSA the
daemon talks to the kernel device /dev/sfspeaker-safety, whose contract is
../include/sf_speaker_safety.h. The split is the one of Linux: the kernel holds the
four speaker volumes at -100 dB unless this daemon owns the device and pings it at
least every 250 ms while the speakers play; the daemon models the voice coil
temperature from the current/voltage sense stream and lowers the volumes. Any error
is a panic: the process exits (status 101), the device closes, the kernel locks.
There is no option that bypasses the model or the lease.

Build
-----
  ./build.sh            arm64 Mach-O for the guest -> build/speakersafetyd
                        (host cargo --offline --locked, target aarch64-apple-darwin,
                        linked by darwin-linker.py with the lab's pinned cross
                        toolchain, SDK and libSystem; checks the header's ioctl
                        numbers and that only libSystem is needed)
  ./run-host-tests.sh   host tests on x86_64 Linux (needs alsa-lib's libasound.so.2)

Crates (all from the local cargo registry, ~/.cargo/registry, no network):
clap 4.6.7 (derive), log 0.4.34, signal-hook 0.4.4, libc 0.2.190 and what they pull
in (Cargo.lock). Not available offline and replaced by small files here:
configparser -> src/ini.rs, simple_logger and clap-verbosity-flag -> src/logger.rs.
alsa is replaced by src/device.rs.

Command line
------------
  speakersafetyd [-c DIR] [--machine NAME] [--runtime-dir DIR] [-m DB] [-v...] [-q...]

  -c, --config-path DIR   config base directory; the file read is DIR/apple/j293.conf
                          (default <PREFIX>/share/speakersafetyd; build.sh sets
                          PREFIX=/usr). Install conf/apple/j293.conf there.
      --machine NAME      "apple,j293" or "j293". Without it: sysctl hw.target, then
                          hw.model ("J293AP" or "MacBookPro17,1" give apple,j293).
      --runtime-dir DIR   directory of speakersafetyd.flag (default
                          /var/run/speakersafetyd, created if missing). No flag file:
                          cold start (coil assumed at 109 C). Flag file present, or
                          not writable: warm start (coil assumed at 130 C, the gain
                          starts reduced). The directory must be emptied at boot.
  -m, --max-reduction DB  as upstream: panic if the gain goes below -DB (debugging)
  -v / -q                 more / less logging (stdout; panics go to stderr)
  SIGQUIT                 as upstream: panic at once ("SIGQUIT received")

Must run as root, one instance (the device has one opener). Upstream is restarted by
systemd (Restart=on-failure, RestartSec=1); something has to do that here too, the
daemon ends on every error on purpose.

Linux -> Darwin
---------------
  ALSA ctl device hw:AppleJ293        /dev/sfspeaker-safety (open, O_RDWR)
  ELEM_LOCK of the controls           the open itself (one opener; close = lock)
  write "Speaker Volume Unlock"       SFSPK_IOC_PING with 0xdec1be15; any error,
    = 0xdec1be15                      ETIMEDOUT included, is a panic as on Linux
  read "Speaker Sample Rate"          sfspk_info.sample_rate (SFSPK_IOC_INFO)
  "<spk> Amp Gain Volume": set to     not set (the kernel fixes it); read:
    max, read back                    sfspk_info.amp_gain, dBV = 11 + 0.5 * value
  write "<spk> Speaker Playback       SFSPK_IOC_SET_VOLUME; the speaker is found by
    Volume"                           name (Left Front, Right Front, Left Rear, Right
                                      Rear = 0..3), the other three values are passed
                                      back as SFSPK_IOC_INFO reports them
  dB -> control value (alsa-lib,      same arithmetic, in helpers.rs: dB = -100.5 +
    snd_ctl_convert_from_dB, range    0.5 * value, range 0..sfspk_info.volume_max,
    read from the control each time)  NEAREST step, ties up (see below)
  "<spk> VSENSE/ISENSE Switch" = on   nothing (always on in the kernel)
  sense PCM hw:AppleJ293,2, readi()   read() on the device until `period` frames
    of `period` frames, S16LE, 8 ch   (4096) of 8 S16LE samples are there
  ESTRPIPE (suspend) -> reopen PCM    dropped (no such state, no such errno)
  (none)                              sfspk_info.tripped != 0 or version != 1: panic
  /proc/device-tree/compatible        --machine, or sysctl hw.target / hw.model
  /run/speakersafetyd/...flag         /var/run/speakersafetyd/speakersafetyd.flag
    (systemd RuntimeDirectory)        (--runtime-dir; the daemon makes the directory)
  uclamp_max = 64 (sched_setattr)     ignored with a log line: XNU has no utilization
                                      clamp, and no QoS class is set in its place
  SIGQUIT flag, no SA_RESTART         the same (signal-hook + sigaction)

Rounding: upstream passes alsa::Round::Floor, but alsa-lib only rounds down for a
negative direction; for 0 it rounds to the nearest step. Measured with the host's
alsa-lib 1.2.16: 0 dB -> 201, -12.03 dB -> 177 (-12.0 dB, not 176), -100 dB -> 1.
The port does the same.

Files
-----
  src/main.rs types.rs helpers.rs uclamp.rs   upstream's files, ported; each says in
                                              its header what was changed
  src/device.rs ini.rs logger.rs              new (device, config parser, logger)
  conf/apple/j293.conf                        upstream's, unchanged
  tests/model.rs, tests/process.rs            host tests; tests/common: fake device,
                                              synthetic sense data
  tests/upstream-ref/                         reference for the tests: upstream's
                                              types.rs and helpers.rs UNMODIFIED,
                                              built against a stand-in alsa crate
                                              whose dB conversions call the real
                                              alsa-lib

Not verified (needs the guest, the kernel side and hardware)
------------------------------------------------------------
- Anything against the real device: the open, the three ioctls, the read, the lease
  timing. One period is 85 ms at 48 kHz against a 250 ms lease, as on Linux, but the
  scheduling of the guest is untested (no uclamp, no priority is set).
- That the kernel's sense stream has the scale and channel order the Linux PCM has
  (is_scale/vs_scale of j293.conf); the model is only as good as that.
- What hw.target / hw.model say in the guest (use --machine j293 until known), and
  that /var/run is empty after a boot (if not, every start is a warm start: safe,
  but the gain starts at about -12 dB and recovers over a few minutes of playback).
- The binary has not been run: it is linked against the lab's libSystem only
  (unwinding, sigaction, sysctlbyname and the rest resolve at link time).
- alsa::Round::Floor = 0 is from memory of the alsa crate (its source is not in the
  offline registry). If it were negative, upstream would round down and be up to
  one step (0.5 dB) lower than this port.
